this is what it might look like the car  of the future fast flashy and with some  very eye-catching curves dubbed the  Kozma Tron it's the work of Paul bacon  from Britain he decided to come up with  the car because conventional rides were  too boring  this this would be the kind of car that  I'd be driving in line 2013 and this  would be how the future would be but you  know you get here and it's it's very  disappointing because this isn't what  cars are like and probably for a good  reason but yeah I thought I'm gonna I'm  gonna I'm just gonna make that happen  the cosmo tron takes its inspiration  from old sci-fi movies it's futuristic  the way the future was imagined back in  the 1950s and 60s the engine sticks up  through the bonnet like a rocket and the  driver sits under a bubble made of  plexiglass  I think the view of the future  from the viewpoint of the early 1960s in  the 1950s it's just a very optimistic  view of what the future would be like  what with you know the moon landings and  the space race and I tried to build the  car with that view of the future and  without maybe our view of the future  today the cosmo tron was put together in  paul Bacon's garden shed here in the  City of Leicester normally bacon toils  away he using spare parts to make  souped-up bicycles which he later rents  or sells right now he's working on a  lowrider a bike that's built close to  the ground my first world of my my dad  when I was when I was 12 I learned to  weld then and ever since then I've  realized that you know a bicycle can  easily be chopped around in something  wild in about the space of a day  so unlike the car that takes 18 months  you can you can get that big sort of  reaction on the street in about 12 hours  you know the space-age Kozma Tron gets  that big reaction when bacon takes it  out for a spin through downtown less up  every time he parks he's sure to draw a  crowd  it's the most amazing thing I've ever  seen it's come straight out of 1970s  scooby-doo isn't it Ruby isn't very much  like a scooby-doo car I've never seen  anything like it must have taken five  thousand hours to make that'd be quite  nice that was spinning it see what it  was like yeah it's a cool idea it's  different idea and retro is cool say but  with a modern twist see I like that idea  very much the technology in the Kozma  Tron also has a modern twist to it the  chassis of a BMW z3 from 1998 forms the  base  it took bacon 18 months and plenty of  fiberglass and recycled materials to  transform it into his dream machine I  wanted the whole BMW package so the  engine the gearbox the suspension and  I've not touched any of it it's just  pretty much how it came from the factory  but then I wanted the the flamboyant  design on top of that and yeah it just  provided you know the perfect base the  leftover parts will reuse - of course  for a custom made bicycle there was bits  left over from that one of the bits  being the space saver  tire so I cut the center out of it  mounted it on the back you know it says  it's good for 50 kilometers an hour so  I'm presuming it's all going to be okay  it's got two foot brakes it doesn't go  that fast highly impractical bike but  you know it looks cool  however the Kozma Tron is perfectly  roadworthy and boasts the top speed of  over 200 kilometers an hour now Paul  bacon just needs to find a buyer someone  who will appreciate the Kozma Tron's  unique appeal
 

